  "_exec_summary": [
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000120845,
      "SummaryText": "s Coop has several key elements to their accident prevention program:\n1.  The anhydrous ammonia system adheres to the laws and regulations of the State of Missouri\n2.  Training is required every 3 years, of all personnel on handling, transferring, or transporting anhydrous ammonia.  The training includes properties of ammonia, safe handling practices, first aid, and emergency procedures\n3.  Use of good management practices as specified by industry standards and training aids.\n4.  Frequent site inspections by management and safety consultants/ Insurance Co. Risk Management personnel \n5.  Annual inspections include safety equipment, tanks, and handling equipment.\n6.  Use of proper anhydrous ammonia personal protective equipment and safety equipment.\n\nChanges are made in the system when necessary or when required by regulation change.  Periodic evaluations of the procedures help to determine whether or not a change in operations should be made to provide increased safety to the operators and to the community."
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000120845,
      "SummaryText": "Chula Farmers Coop\nChula, MO\n\nRisk Management Plan - Executive Summary\n\nThe accidental release prevention policy at Chula Farmers Coop involves, but is not limited to technologies, procedures, and management practices. This facility conforms to the laws and regulations of the state of Missouri.  Our emergency response policy involves our cooperation with community response plans that are tailored to our facility and to the emergency response services available in our community and is in compliance with the EPA Emergency Response Program requirements.\n\nThis facility is in the business of selling anhydrous ammonia to farm customers as a form of nitrogen fertilizer.  Storage tanks, nurse tanks, risers for filling nurse tanks, electrical panels, lighting, and safety equipment are all parts of this facilities anhydrous ammonia operation.  Though the facility is manned through out the year, the ammonia is sold to farmers only during spring and fall use seasons.  The facility operates with normal business hours.\n\nThe off-site consequences analysis includes two anhydrous ammonia release scenarios, identified as A\u00bfworst-caseA\u00bf release and A\u00bfalternativeA\u00bf release scenarios.  The first scenario is defined by EPA and shall assume the maximum quantity of the largest vessel is released as a gas in 10 minutes due to a catastrophic break.  The alternative release scenario is defined as a more likely accidental release of anhydrous ammonia caused by a transfer hose failure.\n\nThe results of the above off-site consequences analysis have been pulled from the executive summary but can be found in Tab 2 of the facilities' Risk Management Plan.  These analyses have been documented in the facility's Risk Management Program.\n\nChula Farmers Coop has had no anhydrous ammonia leaks within the past five years.  Even though the company has had no releases it still coordinates annual visits with the local fire department to review the systems and procedures for responding to a leak.\n\nChula Farmer"
    }
  ]
